"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","13","**SpiderFoot** is an open source intelligence (OSINT) automation tool. It integrates with just about every data source available and utilises a range of methods for data analysis, making that data easy to navigate. "
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","15","SpiderFoot has an embedded web-server for providing a clean and intuitive web-based interface but can also be used completely via the command-line.  It's written in **Python 3** and **GPL-licensed**."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","17","<img src=""https://www.spiderfoot.net/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/opensource-screenshot-v4.png"" />"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","38","Need more from SpiderFoot? Check out [SpiderFoot HX](https://www.spiderfoot.net/hx) for:"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","50","- Bring your own Python SpiderFoot modules"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","53","See the full set of differences between SpiderFoot HX and the open source version [here](https://www.spiderfoot.net/open-source-vs-hx/)."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","57","SpiderFoot can be used offensively (e.g. in a red team exercise or penetration test) for reconnaissance of your target or defensively to gather information about what you or your organisation might have exposed over the Internet."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","59","You can target the following entities in a SpiderFoot scan:"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","72","SpiderFoot's 200+ modules feed each other in a publisher/subscriber model to ensure maximum data extraction to do things like:"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","93","To install and run SpiderFoot, you need at least Python 3.7 and a number of Python libraries which you can install with `pip`. We recommend you install a packaged release since master will often have bleeding edge features and modules that aren't fully tested."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","98","$ wget https://github.com/smicallef/spiderfoot/archive/v4.0.tar.gz"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","100","$ cd spiderfoot-4.0"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","108","$ git clone https://github.com/smicallef/spiderfoot.git"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","109","$ cd spiderfoot"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","114","Check out the [documentation](https://www.spiderfoot.net/documentation) and our [asciinema videos](https://asciinema.org/~spiderfoot) for more tutorials."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","118","Whether you're a contributor, user or just curious about SpiderFoot and OSINT in general, we'd love to have you join our community! SpiderFoot now has a [Discord server](https://discord.gg/vyvztrG) for seeking help from the community, requesting features or just general OSINT chit-chat."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","122","We have a comprehensive write-up and reference of the correlation rule-set introduced in SpiderFoot 4.0 [here](/correlations/README.md)."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","128","SpiderFoot has over 200 modules, most of which *don't require API keys*, and many of those that do require API keys *have a free tier*."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","313","[Steven Black Hosts](https://github.com/StevenBlack/hosts)|Check if a domain is malicious (malware or adware) according to Steven Black Hosts list.|Free API"
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","368","Read more at the [project website](https://www.spiderfoot.net/r.php?u=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuc3BpZGVyZm9vdC5uZXQv&s=os_gh), including more complete documentation, blog posts with tutorials/guides, plus information about [SpiderFoot HX](https://www.spiderfoot.net/r.php?u=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuc3BpZGVyZm9vdC5uZXQvaHgvCg==&s=os_gh)."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\README.md","370","Latest updates announced on [Twitter](https://twitter.com/spiderfoot)."
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","2","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\.github\ISSUE_TEMPLATE.md"",""7"",""What version of SpiderFoot are you using (stable release or Git `master` branch)?"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","3","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\.github\ISSUE_TEMPLATE.md"",""11"",""* https://github.com/smicallef/spiderfoot/issues"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","4","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""3"",""SpiderFoot’s goal is to automate OSINT collection and analysis to the greatest extent possible. Since its inception, SpiderFoot has heavily focused on automating OSINT collection and entity extraction, but the automation of common analysis tasks -- beyond some reporting and visualisations -- has been left entirely to the user. The meant that the strength of SpiderFoot's data collection capabilities has sometimes been its weakness since with so much data collected, users have often needed to export it and use other tools to weed out data of interest."""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","5","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""7"",""We started tackling this analysis gap with the launch of SpiderFoot HX in 2019 through the introduction of the """"Correlations"""" feature. This feature was represented by some 30 """"correlation rules"""" that ran with each scan, analyzing data and presenting results reflecting SpiderFoot's opinionated view on what may be important or interesting. Here are a few of those rules as examples:"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","6","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""14"",""With the release of SpiderFoot 4.0 we wanted to bring this capability from SpiderFoot HX to the community, but also re-imagine it at the same time so that the community might not simply run rules we provide, but also write their own correlation rules and contribute them back. We also hope that just as with modules, we see a long list of contributions made in the years ahead so that all may benefit."""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","7","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""24"",""The simplest way to think of a SpiderFoot correlation rule is like a simple database query that consists of a few sections:"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","8","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""32"",""Here's an example rule that looks at SpiderFoot scan results for data revealing open TCP ports where the banner (the data returned upon connecting to the port) reports a software version. It does so by applying some regular expressions to the content of `TCP_PORT_OPEN_BANNER` data elements, filtering out some false positives and then grouping the results by the banner itself  so that one correlation result is created per banner revealing a version:"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","9","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""67"",""-> # python3.9 ./sf.py -s www.binarypool.com -m sfp_dnsresolve,sfp_portscan_tcp            """
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","10","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""68"",""2022-04-06 08:14:58,476 [INFO] sflib : Scan [94EB5F0B] for 'www.binarypool.com' initiated."""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","11","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""77"",""<img src=""""https://www.spiderfoot.net/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/sf4correlations.png"""" />"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","12","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""82"",""In short, SpiderFoot translates the YAML rules into a combination queries against the backend database of scan results and Python logic to filter and group the results, creating """"correlation results"""" in the SpiderFoot database. These results can be viewed in the SpiderFot web interface or from the SpiderFoot CLI. You can also query them directly out of the SQLite database if you like (they are in the `tbl_scan_correlation_results` table, and the `tbl_scan_correlation_results_events` table maps the events (data elements) to the correlation result)."""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","13","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""86"",""Each rule exists as a YAML file within the `/correlations` folder in the SpiderFoot installation path. Here you can see a list of rules in 4.0, which we hope to grow over time:"""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","14","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""119"",""To create your own rule, simply copy the `template.yaml` file in the `correlations` folder to a meaningful name that matches the ID you intend to provide it, e.g. `aws_cloud_usage.yaml` and edit the rule to fit your needs. Save it and re-start SpiderFoot for the rule to be loaded. If there are any syntax errors, SpiderFoot will abort at startup and (hopefully) give you enough information to know where the error is."""
"S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\scratch_search_results.csv","15","""S:\ponisha\spiderfoot-4.0\correlations\README.md"",""135"",""**collection**: A correlation rule contains one or more `collect` blocks. Each `collect` block contains one or more `method` blocks telling SpiderFoot what criteria to use for extracting data from the database and how to filter it down. """
